1
00:00:00,281 --> 00:00:03,089
Last video ended here, with a robot vacuum

2
00:00:03,209 --> 00:00:04,413
at one end of a hallway.

3
00:00:05,073 --> 00:00:07,694
Its displacement, x centimetres from where it starts,

4
00:00:08,215 --> 00:00:11,376
is t cubed minus nine t squared plus

5
00:00:11,477 --> 00:00:13,598
twenty-four t, after t seconds.

6
00:00:14,266 --> 00:00:14,756
Watch it move.

7
00:00:15,640 --> 00:00:16,820
It heads along the hallway,

8
00:00:17,722 --> 00:00:18,061
stops,

9
00:00:18,782 --> 00:00:19,442
backs up a little,

10
00:00:20,083 --> 00:00:20,743
stops again, then

11
00:00:21,384 --> 00:00:21,663
speeds off.

12
00:00:23,408 --> 00:00:26,000
At two and a half seconds, its acceleration

13
00:00:26,101 --> 00:00:27,386
turns out to be negative.

14
00:00:27,970 --> 00:00:29,237
So is it slowing down?

15
00:00:32,990 --> 00:00:33,556
Let's find out.

16
00:00:34,427 --> 00:00:37,577
Displacement is position measured from a fixed origin,

17
00:00:37,920 --> 00:00:39,123
here where the robot started,

18
00:00:39,645 --> 00:00:40,929
and it's negative on the left.

19
00:00:41,652 --> 00:00:45,020
Velocity is how fast displacement changes, so it's

20
00:00:45,061 --> 00:00:47,046
the gradient of the displacement graph:

21
00:00:47,728 --> 00:00:49,673
v equals d x by d t.

22
00:00:50,495 --> 00:00:53,919
Differentiate, and v is three t squared minus

23
00:00:54,061 --> 00:00:55,882
eighteen t plus twenty-four,

24
00:00:56,464 --> 00:00:59,287
which factors to three, t minus two, t

25
00:00:59,328 --> 00:00:59,968
minus four.

26
00:01:00,691 --> 00:01:02,574
So the robot is at rest at two

27
00:01:02,615 --> 00:01:05,180
seconds and at four seconds, where the graph

28
00:01:05,201 --> 00:01:05,581
is flat.

29
00:01:06,224 --> 00:01:08,891
In between, v is negative, and the robot

30
00:01:08,933 --> 00:01:09,553
moves left.

31
00:01:10,337 --> 00:01:12,923
Speed is the size of the velocity, without

32
00:01:12,965 --> 00:01:13,365
its sign.

33
00:01:14,216 --> 00:01:16,792
Acceleration is how fast the velocity changes.

34
00:01:17,296 --> 00:01:19,807
So it's the derivative of velocity, which is

35
00:01:19,868 --> 00:01:21,515
the second derivative of displacement.

36
00:01:22,158 --> 00:01:24,522
We write it as d two x by

37
00:01:24,703 --> 00:01:25,624
d t squared,

38
00:01:26,406 --> 00:01:28,188
or as x with two dots on top,

39
00:01:28,610 --> 00:01:29,912
said x double dot.

40
00:01:30,675 --> 00:01:33,484
Here, x double dot is six t minus

41
00:01:33,605 --> 00:01:36,414
eighteen, in centimetres per second, per second.

42
00:01:37,118 --> 00:01:40,197
It's negative before three seconds, and positive after.

43
00:01:40,900 --> 00:01:43,023
At two seconds the robot is at rest,

44
00:01:43,385 --> 00:01:46,350
but its acceleration is minus six, so it's

45
00:01:46,391 --> 00:01:47,652
about to start moving left.

46
00:01:48,279 --> 00:01:48,951
Now the hook.

47
00:01:49,535 --> 00:01:51,762
At two and a half seconds, v is

48
00:01:52,044 --> 00:01:55,654
minus nine quarters, and the acceleration is minus

49
00:01:55,675 --> 00:01:55,935
three.

50
00:01:56,658 --> 00:01:59,098
The velocity is already negative, and a negative

51
00:01:59,139 --> 00:02:02,620
acceleration pushes it further below zero, so the

52
00:02:02,661 --> 00:02:03,321
speed grows,

53
00:02:03,842 --> 00:02:05,742
from two and a quarter centimetres per second

54
00:02:06,023 --> 00:02:07,003
towards three.

55
00:02:07,725 --> 00:02:09,830
The robot is speeding up, even though its

56
00:02:09,871 --> 00:02:11,133
acceleration is negative.

57
00:02:11,897 --> 00:02:13,112
Compare one second.

58
00:02:13,677 --> 00:02:15,239
There, v is nine,

59
00:02:15,801 --> 00:02:17,945
a is minus twelve, and the robot is

60
00:02:18,006 --> 00:02:19,748
slowing down as it heads for rest.

61
00:02:20,370 --> 00:02:22,917
So when velocity and acceleration have the same

62
00:02:22,998 --> 00:02:24,301
sign, it speeds up.

63
00:02:24,945 --> 00:02:26,611
Opposite signs, it slows down.

64
00:02:27,208 --> 00:02:30,283
Velocity is a rate, so last video's idea

65
00:02:30,384 --> 00:02:31,167
works here too.

66
00:02:31,831 --> 00:02:34,818
Integrating velocity over an interval gives the change

67
00:02:34,920 --> 00:02:35,621
in displacement.

68
00:02:36,384 --> 00:02:38,671
From zero to six seconds, that's x of

69
00:02:38,752 --> 00:02:41,762
six minus x of zero, which is thirty-six

70
00:02:41,783 --> 00:02:42,263
centimetres.

71
00:02:43,068 --> 00:02:45,364
But the robot didn't just travel thirty-six centimetres.

72
00:02:45,788 --> 00:02:48,797
It went right twenty, back four, then right

73
00:02:48,858 --> 00:02:52,008
another twenty, so it covered forty-four centimetres in

74
00:02:52,069 --> 00:02:52,429
total.

75
00:02:53,173 --> 00:02:54,949
The integral gives the displacement.

76
00:02:55,595 --> 00:02:57,740
For the distance, add the size of each

77
00:02:57,801 --> 00:02:59,365
leg, between the rest times.

78
00:03:00,088 --> 00:03:01,475
We can also go the other way.

79
00:03:01,981 --> 00:03:03,017
Here's a different particle.

80
00:03:03,560 --> 00:03:06,566
Its acceleration is six t minus four, and

81
00:03:06,587 --> 00:03:08,149
it starts at rest at the origin.

82
00:03:08,752 --> 00:03:10,538
Find where it is at two seconds.

83
00:03:11,502 --> 00:03:14,327
Integrate the acceleration to get velocity: three t

84
00:03:14,368 --> 00:03:16,813
squared minus four t, plus a constant.

85
00:03:17,555 --> 00:03:19,900
Integration only knows the shape, not where it

86
00:03:19,962 --> 00:03:20,362
starts,

87
00:03:20,864 --> 00:03:22,187
so we need the starting value.

88
00:03:22,849 --> 00:03:25,573
At rest at t equals zero means v

89
00:03:25,734 --> 00:03:28,218
is zero there, so the constant is zero.

90
00:03:28,980 --> 00:03:29,662
Integrate again:

91
00:03:30,246 --> 00:03:32,776
x is t cubed minus two t squared,

92
00:03:32,918 --> 00:03:34,122
plus another constant.

93
00:03:34,725 --> 00:03:36,932
Starting at the origin makes that zero too.

94
00:03:37,576 --> 00:03:40,101
Two steps up need two constants, so we

95
00:03:40,122 --> 00:03:41,525
need two starting values.

96
00:03:42,175 --> 00:03:44,842
At two seconds, x is eight minus eight,

97
00:03:44,923 --> 00:03:45,564
which is zero.

98
00:03:46,127 --> 00:03:47,875
The particle is back where it started.

99
00:03:48,481 --> 00:03:49,372
But it didn't sit still.

100
00:03:50,061 --> 00:03:52,850
Its velocity, three t squared minus four t,

101
00:03:53,293 --> 00:03:55,701
is negative until four thirds of a second.

102
00:03:56,344 --> 00:03:59,350
So first it moves left, to minus thirty-two

103
00:03:59,571 --> 00:04:00,893
over twenty-seven of a metre,

104
00:04:01,476 --> 00:04:02,978
then it turns and comes back.

105
00:04:03,701 --> 00:04:07,068
Its displacement is zero, but it travelled sixty-four

106
00:04:07,210 --> 00:04:08,332
over twenty-seven metres.

107
00:04:09,081 --> 00:04:11,308
The same calculus works for any quantity that

108
00:04:11,368 --> 00:04:12,371
changes with time.

109
00:04:12,952 --> 00:04:14,735
At a ski field, the snow is one

110
00:04:14,836 --> 00:04:17,420
hundred centimetres deep, and it's building up at

111
00:04:17,500 --> 00:04:20,165
twenty minus two t centimetres per day.

112
00:04:20,706 --> 00:04:22,759
So d D by d t is twenty

113
00:04:22,819 --> 00:04:23,503
minus two t.

114
00:04:24,248 --> 00:04:25,603
Integrate, and use the start.

115
00:04:26,129 --> 00:04:28,936
D is one hundred, plus twenty t, minus

116
00:04:28,977 --> 00:04:29,597
t squared.

117
00:04:30,340 --> 00:04:32,508
The second derivative is minus two, so the

118
00:04:32,529 --> 00:04:34,957
build-up rate drops by two centimetres per day,

119
00:04:35,219 --> 00:04:35,640
every day.

120
00:04:36,403 --> 00:04:38,466
At day five, for example, d D by

121
00:04:38,507 --> 00:04:40,629
d t is ten, so the snow is

122
00:04:40,670 --> 00:04:43,093
deepening at ten centimetres per day, but that

123
00:04:43,194 --> 00:04:44,515
rise is slowing down.

124
00:04:45,217 --> 00:04:46,802
The snow is deepest when the rate is

125
00:04:46,883 --> 00:04:49,835
zero, at day ten, with two hundred centimetres.

126
00:04:50,519 --> 00:04:52,633
After that, the rate is negative, and the

127
00:04:52,674 --> 00:04:53,177
snow melts.

128
00:04:53,798 --> 00:04:54,934
Here's the chain to remember.

129
00:04:55,499 --> 00:04:58,338
Displacement, velocity, acceleration.

130
00:04:59,123 --> 00:05:00,851
Differentiate to go down the chain.

131
00:05:01,535 --> 00:05:02,900
Integrate to go back up,

132
00:05:03,563 --> 00:05:05,831
and pay one starting value for each step

133
00:05:05,912 --> 00:05:06,012
up.

134
00:05:08,724 --> 00:05:09,148
Your turn.

135
00:05:09,892 --> 00:05:13,099
A particle's displacement is x equals t cubed

136
00:05:13,420 --> 00:05:16,366
minus six t squared plus nine t metres,

137
00:05:16,847 --> 00:05:19,273
for t at least zero, in seconds.

138
00:05:19,935 --> 00:05:22,315
Find its acceleration when it's first at rest.

139
00:05:22,861 --> 00:05:23,835
Pause here to answer.

140
00:05:34,400 --> 00:05:37,106
Velocity is three t squared minus twelve t

141
00:05:37,166 --> 00:05:39,912
plus nine, which is three, t minus one,

142
00:05:39,932 --> 00:05:40,955
t minus three.

143
00:05:41,778 --> 00:05:43,613
It's first at rest at one second.

144
00:05:44,399 --> 00:05:47,247
Acceleration is six t minus twelve, so at

145
00:05:47,407 --> 00:05:49,874
one second it's minus six metres per second

146
00:05:49,894 --> 00:05:50,174
squared.

147
00:05:50,800 --> 00:05:51,670
Zero is wrong.

148
00:05:52,339 --> 00:05:54,575
Being at rest doesn't mean the acceleration is

149
00:05:54,616 --> 00:05:54,938
zero.

150
00:05:56,570 --> 00:05:56,972
Last one.

151
00:05:57,678 --> 00:06:00,460
A particle has acceleration x double dot equals

152
00:06:00,622 --> 00:06:01,882
four minus six t.

153
00:06:02,725 --> 00:06:04,931
It starts at x equals one metre, with

154
00:06:04,972 --> 00:06:06,617
velocity two metres per second.

155
00:06:07,421 --> 00:06:10,033
Find its displacement when t equals two.

156
00:06:10,718 --> 00:06:11,483
Pause here to answer.

157
00:06:22,034 --> 00:06:25,621
Integrate once: velocity is four t minus three

158
00:06:25,662 --> 00:06:26,903
t squared, plus C,

159
00:06:27,485 --> 00:06:29,689
and the start gives C equals two.

160
00:06:30,451 --> 00:06:31,051
Integrate again:

161
00:06:31,693 --> 00:06:34,537
x is two t squared minus t cubed,

162
00:06:34,719 --> 00:06:37,963
plus two t, plus another constant, and starting

163
00:06:38,004 --> 00:06:39,366
at one metre makes it one.

164
00:06:40,289 --> 00:06:42,918
At t equals two, that's eight minus eight,

165
00:06:43,079 --> 00:06:45,969
plus four, plus one, which is five metres.

166
00:06:46,776 --> 00:06:47,809
Four metres is wrong.

167
00:06:48,135 --> 00:06:49,449
It forgets the starting position.

168
00:06:51,088 --> 00:06:54,782
So displacement, velocity and acceleration are linked by

169
00:06:54,863 --> 00:06:56,448
calculus in both directions.

170
00:06:57,113 --> 00:06:58,302
That's the end of calculus for now.

171
00:06:58,886 --> 00:06:59,831
Next, a new topic.

172
00:07:00,714 --> 00:07:03,175
Here are the second particle's positions at one,

173
00:07:03,576 --> 00:07:05,577
two, three and four seconds:

174
00:07:06,258 --> 00:07:09,059
minus one, zero, nine, thirty-two.

175
00:07:10,062 --> 00:07:12,108
A list of numbers in order, like this,

176
00:07:12,249 --> 00:07:13,332
is called a sequence.

177
00:07:14,076 --> 00:07:15,784
What patterns can a list like this have?

178
00:07:18,555 --> 00:07:19,438
Here are the key notes.

179
00:07:19,961 --> 00:07:21,729
Pause the video if you need more time

180
00:07:21,750 --> 00:07:22,231
to copy them.
